  {
   "id": "PR_T1",
   "type": "proof",
   "name": null,
   "statement": "Consider any arbitrary bidder \\(i\\) with valuation \\(v_i\\) and the bids \\(b_i\\) , \\(b_{j}\\) be bid of the other bidders. Define \\(B = \\max_{j \\neq i} b_j\\) as the highest bid by any other bidder. \\(\\epsilon\\) be the least increment value in the auction. For bidder \\(i\\) to have a dominant strategy, \\(b_i\\) must maximize \\(\\text{Utility}(b_i)\\) for all possible \\(b_{j}\\). However, if \\(b_i > B\\) lead to winning with a positive utility, Utility\\((b_i)\\) = \\(v_i - b_i\\), which is maximized precisely when \\(b_i\\) approaches \\(B\\) from above. Since there always exist a \\(b_i' = B - \\epsilon\\) could lead to a higher utility when \\(v_i \\ge B\\). Utility\\((b_i')\\) = \\(v_i - B + \\epsilon\\) which is greater than Utility\\((b_i)\\) = \\(v_i - b_i\\) if \\(b_i = v_i\\). Thus, there is no single \\(b_i\\) that always lead to the highest utility, proving that no dominant strategy exists.",
   "origin": "paper",
   "tags": [],
   "section": "5 Example",
   "label": null,
   "proves": [
    "T1"
   ]
  },
